  "notes": [
    "All six stars—SDSS J113102.87+665751.1 (Pal14 ID 4), SDSS J064337.13+291410.0 (Pal14 ID 7), SDSS J172630.60+075544.0 (Pal14 ID 10), SDSS J095816.39+005224.4 (Pal14 ID 15), SDSS J074728.84+185520.4 (Pal14 ID 16), and SDSS J064257.02+371604.2 (Pal14 ID 17)—were selected for follow-up as prior Pal14 HVS candidates. The paper's final finding at T:p7-b8 is that all are bound to the Milky Way, so none qualifies under candidates_found; the possible origins in Table 6 (I:p10-b0) (runaway, heated, or accreted) are not findings of Galactic unbound status. The supplied material provides Gaia DR2 proper motions but no Gaia source IDs, so only SDSS and Pal14 identifiers are recorded.",
    "SDSS J113102.87+665751.1: RA and Dec are transcribed from the SDSS designation J113102.87+665751.1 (T:p2-b5); the paper does not otherwise tabulate coordinates or a parallax for this star. The paper states that all six program stars are bound to the Milky Way but does not give numerical bound/unbound probabilities. Table 5 lists orbital r_min = 0.90 ± 0.65 kpc and r_max = 8.71 ± 0.20 kpc (T:p6-b1), also repeated as r_min = 0.90 kpc and r_max = 8.71 kpc in §5.1 (T:p9-b10); these are orbital minimum/maximum galactocentric distances, not the present-day galactocentric radius, so they are not reported as galactocentric_radius. The Table 5 distance d = 1.14 kpc has no tabulated uncertainty; the text quotes a general 15–20% distance uncertainty (T:p5-b9).",
    "SDSS J064337.13+291410.0: Target is SDSS J064337.13+291410.0 = Pal14 ID 7. At T:p6-b0 the Ziegerer et al. (2015) proper-motion entries for this star are ellipses, so no value from that study is listed. No independent equatorial RA/Dec or numerical parallax is tabulated; the SDSS designation encodes J2000 coordinates but is not treated here as an astrometric measurement. The text at T:p7-b8 states that all program stars are bound to the Milky Way but gives no numerical per-star bound or unbound probability, so no probability value is listed. The adopted distance at T:p6-b1 is 3.16 kpc; the global 15–20% distance uncertainty described at T:p5-b9 is not tabulated for this star.",
    "SDSS J172630.60+075544.0: RA and Dec are decoded from the SDSS identifier \"J172630.60+075544.0\" in T:p6-b1 (also T:p2-b5); the paper provides no separate coordinate table. No parallax is reported for the target. The paper states that all program stars are bound to the MW (T:p7-b8, T:p10-b18) but gives no numerical bound or unbound probability, so none is entered. Table 5 r_min, r_max, and Z_max (T:p6-b1; I:p6-b1) are orbital extrema, not the present-day galactocentric radius, so galactocentric_radius is not entered. The Ziegerer et al. (2015) proper-motion entry for this star is \"...\" (no measurement).",
    "SDSS J095816.39+005224.4: Target identified as SDSS J095816.39+005224.4 (Pal14 ID 15). Table 4 (T:p6-b0 / I:p6-b0) reports three historical proper-motion sets, all retained. The paper does not report explicit RA or Dec values, a parallax, a current Galactocentric radius, or a numerical bound/unbound probability for this star. It states that all six program stars are bound to the Milky Way (T:p7-b8) without giving a probability. Table 5 (T:p6-b1 / I:p6-b1) gives the single distance 2.19 kpc without a per-star tabulated uncertainty; T:p5-b9 quotes a global 15–20% distance uncertainty. The Table 5 r_min and r_max values are orbital extrema, not the present-day Galactocentric radius, so they were not extracted as galactocentric_radius.",
    "SDSS J074728.84+185520.4: The paper states that all program stars are bound to the Milky Way but does not report a numerical bound or unbound probability for this star, so no probability value is included. The table gives orbital minimum and maximum galactocentric distances (r_min = 10.77 ± 0.71 kpc; r_max = 12.70 ± 1.20 kpc), not a present-day galactocentric radius. The RA and Dec are parsed from the reported SDSS designation because no separate coordinate table is supplied; no coordinate conversion was performed. The distance uncertainty is not given per star; the text states typical distance uncertainties of 15–20% (and about 10% for four Gaia DR2-based distances).",
    "SDSS J064257.02+371604.2: Target: SDSS J064257.02+371604.2 (Pal14 ID 17). The paper does not list an independent RA/Dec astrometric solution; the coordinates above are taken from the SDSS identifier. No numerical bound or unbound probability is reported for this star; the paper only concludes that all six program stars are bound to the Milky Way. Table 5 lists r_min = 8.20 ± 0.18 kpc and r_max = 9.83 ± 0.37 kpc, but these are orbital minimum/maximum Galactocentric distances, not the present-day galactocentric radius, so they were not recorded as galactocentric_radius. The general Table 5 distance uncertainty is stated to be about 15–20%, with no individual uncertainty for this star."
  ]
}
